.TH SYS_RANDOM 2
.SH NAME
sys_random \- unpredictable bytes (SYS_RANDOM)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_random(void *buf, size_t n);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_random
fills
.I buf
with
.I n
unpredictable bytes, at most 4096 per call, for keys, nonces and
passwords' salts. They come from a ChaCha20 generator whose key is
hashed (BLAKE2b) from a pool of sources: the processor's own generator
when present (RDSEED, else RDRAND, on x86-64), the timing jitter of a
small loop at boot, and the time of interrupts. It reseeds at most once a
second, and replaces its key after each call (fast key erasure), so a
later look at the kernel's memory cannot recover earlier output.
.PP
Until enough entropy has been gathered after boot (256 bits, counted
cautiously), the call fails with
.BR EAGAIN ;
try again a moment later.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.IR n ,
or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I n
is above 4096.
.TP
.B EAGAIN
Not seeded yet.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I buf
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR intro (2)
